Steps to Save Output to a File Using fprintf in Octave:

Step 1: Open the file using fopen()
  Syntax: fid = fopen('filename.txt', 'mode');
  Modes:
    'w'  — write (creates new file or overwrites existing)
    'a'  — append (adds to end of existing file)
    'r'  — read only
  Example: fid = fopen('output.txt', 'w');

Step 2: Check if file opened successfully
  if fid == -1
    error('File could not be opened');
  end

Step 3: Write output to the file using fprintf()
  Syntax: fprintf(fid, 'format string', variables);
  Format specifiers:
    %d — integer
    %f — floating point
    %s — string
    %e — scientific notation
    \n — newline character
  Example: fprintf(fid, 'Result = %.4f\n', result);

Step 4: Close the file using fclose()
  Syntax: fclose(fid);
  This is mandatory — it saves and releases the file properly.

Complete Example:
  % Step 1: Open file
  fid = fopen('results.txt', 'w');

  % Step 2: Check file
  if fid == -1
    error('Cannot open file');
  end

  % Step 3: Write data
  x = 3.14159;
  fprintf(fid, 'The value of pi is approximately %.5f\n', x);
  for i = 1:5
    fprintf(fid, 'i = %d, i^2 = %d\n', i, i^2);
  end

  % Step 4: Close file
  fclose(fid);

After running this, a file named results.txt will be created containing all the printed output.

